// A default reduction delays the detection of an error, and the trace is where that delay becomes visible. A state
// whose only action is a reduction performs it for every lookahead rather than only for the lookaheads it was computed
// for, which is what takes the reduce entries out of the table. The price is that a token which is an error is not
// recognized as one until the parser reaches a state which has no default reduction, and the reductions it performs on
// the way happen even though the parse is already doomed.
//
// The chain of single productions is what makes that visible rather than merely true: reducing the heading uncovers a
// state which reduces the section, which uncovers a state which reduces the document, so a single token which cannot
// follow the heading produces three reductions before the error is reported. Without the default reductions the error
// would be reported right after the first token was shifted and none of the three would appear.
//
// The offset the error is reported at is the second word, so the delay moves the reductions but not the position. Every
// backend has to delay by exactly this much: reporting earlier means the default reductions were not applied, reporting
// later means a state which should have refused the token did not.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its only rule is a run of letters.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    NAME: /[a-z]+/;
}

@parser {
    document
        : section
        ;

    section
        : heading
        ;

    heading
        : NAME
        ;
}
